{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-337356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=({x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337356" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337356" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337356" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337356" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=(2x-7)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337276" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{6x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337276" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y={x}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337278" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337278" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y=({x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337274" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337274" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=({x}^{2}-1)\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(4x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337362" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337362" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337362" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337362" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=1-4x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-3x})}^{′}+3(u{e}^{-3x}) & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-3u{e}^{-3x}+3u{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{-3x}} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-3x} & =1-4x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{3x}(1-4x) \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-3x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{-3x} \\\\  & ={e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-3x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{3x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(x){e}^{-3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\int {e}^{3x}(1+4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "\\int {e}^{3x}(4x-1)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{-3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] ["math" "{e}^{3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352895" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{e}^{-3x}\\int {e}^{3x}(1-4x)\\,\\text{d}x"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=6{e}^{-5x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-2x})}^{′}+2(u{e}^{-2x}) & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-2u{e}^{-2x}+2u{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{-2x}} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-2x} & =6{e}^{-5x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6{e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =-2{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-3x}\\cdot {e}^{-2x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-352891" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0