174" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337174" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337174" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-8x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-10x}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337174" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{6x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "u(x){e}^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+2x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "1+x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "4x+2{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "4x+{e}^{-4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x+{e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4x{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "{e}^{2x}+x{e}^{-2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337518" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{-4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337466" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "67cdf95dfdaaf0054a0354b1b923ad1918dfc5ec59afcba14f886315d7dedf25"} {"question" "q-337466" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337466" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "u(x){e}^{-2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{-6x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{2x}"] ["math" "u(x){e}^{4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/