e/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337510" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{2}x{e}^{-4x}"] ["math" "{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}x{e}^{-4x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "q-337510" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6x{e}^{4x}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{-4x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/state.json" "file_sha256" "aae15b114f35311b88b4b61b11483f8a34a8f05ab29de44b9244f2ee3f1aefdd"} {"question" "e-21774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/example-21774.json" "file_sha256" "601864d3543df40f436fdd0752a3f6afcdb590f25e8230541a3305d1fb5840fa"} {"question" "e-21774" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/example-21774.json" "file_sha256" "601864d3543df40f436fdd0752a3f6afcdb590f25e8230541a3305d1fb5840fa"} {"question" "e-21775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/example-21775.json" "file_sha256" "b78cdb5d5674e367e523e06ca3fd1a19425f72274d8b04eaa415f4c988296709"} {"question" "e-21775" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/example-21775.json" "file_sha256" "b78cdb5d5674e367e523e06ca3fd1a19425f72274d8b04eaa415f4c988296709"} {"question" "e-21777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/assets/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/example-21777.json" "file_sha256" "e9b2666d7caa347a36b7f660c5d72a4bd63483d8d630d137e7dcc43f1f8c5dfe"} {"question" "e-21777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14044108/example-21777.json" "file_sha256" "e9b2666d7caa347a36b7f660c5d72a4bd63483d8d630d137e7dcc43f1f8c5dfe"}] "usage" {"q-337179" [] "e-21777" [] "e-21774" [] "q-337175" [] "q-337174" [] "e-21775" []} "basis" 1644 "retractions" [[17592186157696 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 2y = \\sin 4x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAccording to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\nSolving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $y_{p}(x) =$ {{field-3}}."] [17592186157696 :question/answer-fields 17592186155391] [17592186157696 :question/answer-fields 17592186155403] [17592186157695 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = e^{2x}$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAccording to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\nSolving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $y_{p}(x) =$ {{field-3}}."] [17592186157698 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} - 2y = 4\\cosh 2x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAccording to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\nSolving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ {{field-2}}.\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is $y_{p}(x) =$ {{field-3}}."] [17592186156984 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x)$.\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)$?"] [17592186156984 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."] [17592186156985 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$ $AAAAA$."] [17592186156985 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u$, we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(ue^{5x})^{′} - 5(ue^{5x}) &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} - 5ue^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} + 5ue^{5x} - 5ue^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{5x} &= \\cos 2x \\\\\nu^{′} &= e^{-5x}\\cos 2x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= e^{-5x}\\cos 2x \\\\\n∫u^{′}dx &= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx \\\\\nu &= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\ny_p = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= u(x)e^{5x} \\\\\n&= ∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx \\cdot e^{5x} \\\\\n&= e^{5x}∫e^{-5x}\\cos 2xdx\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186156986 :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x$.\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u$, we have $u(x) =$ $AAAAA$.\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$ $AAAAA$."] [17592186156986 :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c$, we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p$, we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u$, we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(ue^{3x})^{′} - 3(ue^{3x}) &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} - 3ue^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} + 3ue^{3x} - 3ue^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′}e^{3x} &= 12\\sinh 3x \\\\\nu^{′} &= 12e^{-3x}\\sinh 3x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= 12e^{-3x}\\sinh 3x \\\\\n&= 12e^{-3x}(\\frac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\\n&= 6e^{-3x}(e^{3x} - e^{-3x}) \\\\\n&= 6 - 6e^{-6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu^{′} &= 6 - 6e^{-6x} \\\\\n∫u^{′}dx &= ∫6 - 6e^{-6x}dx \\\\\nu &= 6x + e^{-6x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\ny_p = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny_{p} &= u(x)e^{3x} \\\\\n&= 6xe^{3x} + e^{-3x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186155374 17592186155375 17592186155376 17592186155377 17592186155378 17592186155380 17592186155381 17592186155382 17592186155383 17592186155384 17592186155386 17592186155387 17592186155388 17592186155389 17592186155390 17592186155392 17592186155393 17592186155394 17592186155395 17592186155396 17592186155398 17592186155399 17592186155400 17592186155401 17592186155402 17592186155404 17592186155405 17592186155406 17592186155407 17592186155408 17592186155428 17592186155429 17592186155430 17592186155431 17592186155432 17592186155434 17592186155435 17592186155436 17592186155437 17592186155439 17592186155440 17592186155441 17592186155442] "immutable_fields" [[17592186155373 :answer-field/id] [17592186155373 :answer-field/key] [17592186155373 :answer-field/type] [17592186155379 :answer-field/id] [17592186155379 :answer-field/key] [17592186155379 :answer-field/type] [17592186155385 :answer-field/id] [17592186155385 :answer-field/key] [17592186155385 :answer-field/type] [17592186155391 :answer-field/id] [17592186155391 :answer-field/key] [17592186155391 :answer-field/type] [17592186155397 :answer-field/id] [17592186155397 :answer-field/key] [17592186155397 :answer-field/type] [17592186155403 :answer-field/id] [17592186155403 :answer-field/ke